@defgroup micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-logical-functions Logical Functions
@brief Functions used to compose logical expressions in tests.

@details
The functions `mt::all_of()`, `mt::any_of()`, and `mt::negate()` combine
multiple conditions, allowing expressive and concise test logic.
`mt::all_of()` and `mt::any_of()` take two or more operands, and
`mt::any_of()` is an inclusive or: it is true also when all the conditions
are true. A chain of the same operation is reported as a single group,
for example `(a and b and c)`.

All the operands are evaluated before the check, so the functions do not
stop at the first operand that decides the result, like the standard
algorithms of the same names. After a `using namespace std`, an
unqualified call with three or more operands of the same type selects the
standard algorithm; call the functions as `mt::all_of()` and
`mt::any_of()`.

The functions have descriptive names, since `and`, `or`, and `not` are
alternative tokens and cannot be used as identifiers. Using these logical
functions allows developers to express compound conditions clearly and
improve readability.

@par Example

@code{.cpp}
ts.test ("Check compound conditions", [] (auto& t) {
  t.expect (mt::all_of (mt::eq (compute_answer (), 42),
                        mt::eq (compute_float (), 42.0),
                        mt::lt (compute_answer (), 43)));
});
@endcode

The overloaded logical operators of the `micro_test_plus::operators`
namespace are deprecated since 5.1.0, in favour of these functions.
